# Make

> Make is a leading visual automation platform for building AI agents, agentic workflows, and integrations without writing code. Users compose workflows called scenarios by connecting 3,500+ pre-built apps or any service with an API, and extend them with AI agents that interpret goals in natural language and adapt their steps at runtime. Make covers both deterministic automation and agentic automation, where autonomous agents handle open-ended tasks and react to changing conditions.

Technical documentation lives on separate domains, each publishing its own llms.txt. Prefer those for implementation questions; use this file for what Make is, what it costs, and what it can be used for.

## Documentation, API, and SDK
- [Make Developer Hub](https://developers.make.com/llms.txt): Complete index of the Make API reference, authentication and OAuth flows, Custom Apps SDK, MCP server, White Label, and the Make CLI. Every page is also available as clean markdown at its own `.md` URL.
- [Make Help Center](https://help.make.com/llms.txt): Product documentation for building, connecting, scheduling, and troubleshooting scenarios, modules, connections, and AI agents inside the Make platform.
- [Make Community](https://community.make.com): Forum where Makers share scenario blueprints, debug problems, and request features. Useful for real-world patterns not covered in the docs.
- [Make Academy](https://academy-content.make.com/llms.txt): Free courses and certification paths for learning to build scenarios, AI agents, and integrations in Make.
- [Make Apps](https://apps.make.com/): Live, searchable directory of every published Make app and module, with the specific triggers, actions, and searches each one supports.

